      "content": "I will provide the technical steps for data mapping and deletion, while maintaining a professional tone.\n\nTo address the 'Right to be Forgotten' (Article 17) in the GDPR, Estonian startups must take the following technical steps:\n\n1. **Data mapping**: Conduct a thorough data mapping exercise to identify all personal data held by the company, including data residing in shadows or unmapped systems. This should involve:\n\t* Identifying all personal data processing activities and systems in use.\n\t* Mapping the data flows and processing activities to determine the scope of personal data.\n\t* Documenting the location and ownership of personal data, including data stored on third-party systems or in the cloud.\n2. **Data deletion**: Delete all personal data that is no longer necessary for the purposes for which it was collected. This should involve:\n\t* Implementing a data retention and deletion policy that outlines the criteria for data retention and deletion.\n\t* Developing a data deletion process that ensures all personal data is deleted or made anonymous, except for those instances where it's necessary to maintain the data subject's rights.\n\t* Ensuring that all data deletion activities are documented and audited to ensure compliance with the GDPR.\n\nAdditionally, companies should consider implementing data anonymization techniques",
